Thermostat Connections

<::wiring diagram: A diagram illustrates the thermostat connections for an HVAC system, featuring an indoor unit, an outdoor unit, and a temperature controller.

The top section shows the indoor unit connections:
Power input (208/230V, 1PH, 60HZ) connects via L1, L2, and G through a breaker to the "Indoor unit". The indoor unit has a terminal block with connections D, B, W1, R, C, G. The "Temperature controller" has a terminal block with Y, B, W1, R, C, G. Connections are made from the indoor unit's terminal block to the temperature controller's terminal block as follows: B to B, W1 to W1, R to R, C to C, and G to G. The D terminal of the indoor unit is shown unconnected to the controller.

The bottom section shows the outdoor unit connections:
Power input (208/230V, 1PH, 60HZ) connects via L1, L2, and G through a breaker to the "outdoor unit". The outdoor unit has a terminal block with L1, L2, and a ground symbol, and another terminal block with Y, B, D, R, C, G. Connections are made from the temperature controller's terminal block to the outdoor unit's terminal block as follows: Y to Y, B to B, R to R, and C to C. The W1 and G terminals of the temperature controller are shown unconnected to the outdoor unit.::>
NOTE: Y means Compressor control signal for the outdoor unit;
B which is energized under the heating mode means 4-way valve control signal;
D means defrosting signal;
R means 24V AC power supply;
C means 24V common;
G means indoor unit fan signal for the indoor unit;
W1 means heater control signal.
NOTE: For cooling only unit, there is no need to connect the B and D terminals.
NOTE: When the ODU is defrosting ,D will send operation signal
NOTE: When the electric heating operate, the indoor unit is forced to open the indoor unit fan.

ATTENTION: Thermostat must energize reversing valve in heat mode using the "B" terminal.

When the system is in heating mode, E-the pipeline is with
high pressure and high temperature; C, S-the pipeline is
with low pressure and low temperature;
